<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.5: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel or Perpendicular to an Axis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00315</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.5: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel or Perpendicular to an Axis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>df6576fd-7a74-4134-8df3-a31cee00a748</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> are shown on the graph.</para>
      
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_12_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      
      <para id="para-00002">Work with a partner to complete the tasks:</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Identify the slope of the original line, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>l</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Undefined</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> through the point (3, -2) and parallel to line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>l</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00006">When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">
                  <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_12_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00009">Then,</para>
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Identify the slope of the new line, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">Undefined</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Write the equation of the new line, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> through the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and perpendicular to line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>l</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00016">When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">
                  <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_12_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00019">Then,</para>
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00020">Identify the slope of the new line, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">0</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00023">Write the equation of the new line, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00025"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00026">The original line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>l</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and the line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> that you created are both parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis. It follows, then, that line n is parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00027">Write the equation of a line that is parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00029"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00030">Write the equation of a line that is perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00033">Write the equation of a line parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00035"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#11-->
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00036">How does the line described as “parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>” relate to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis? Will it always, sometimes, or never be perpendicular to it?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00038">Always perpendicular.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00039">Which is the equation that is perpendicular to the line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> that contains the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para> </note>
    
      <section>
        <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Find Equations of Lines Perpendicular to an Axis</title>
      <para id="para-00040">Lines that are horizontal or vertical are special for two reasons. First, their equations have just one variable.</para>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item>Horizontal line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>; because all the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinates are the same and the slope equals zero.</item>
        <item>Vertical line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>; because all the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinates are the same and the slope is undefined.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00041">Second, they have a special relationship with the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>- and <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-axes because they are either parallel or perpendicular to them.</para>
      <para id="para-00042">Examine the line that passes through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00043">
          <media alt="Line passing through (0,4) and (3,4)">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_14_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>What is the rise?</emphasis> The rise is 0.</para>
      <para><emphasis>What is the run?</emphasis> The run is 3.</para>
      <para><emphasis>What is the slope?</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mtext>rise</m:mtext></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mtext>run</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00045">So, the horizontal line passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> has a slope of 0. For all of the points on this line, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values will always equal 4. So, the equation is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00046">Also notice that it is parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
      <para id="para-00047">Now consider the vertical line passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00048">
          <media alt="Line passing through (3,2) and (3,0)">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_14_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>What is the rise?</emphasis> The rise is 2.</para>
      <para><emphasis>What is the run?</emphasis> The run is 0.</para>
      <para><emphasis>What is the slope?</emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mtext>rise</m:mtext></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mtext>run</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> (undefined)</para>
      
      <para id="para-00050">So, the vertical line passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> has an undefined slope. And, for all of the points on this line, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values will always equal 3. So, the equation is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00051">Also notice that it is parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
      <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>

      <para id="para-00053">Find the equation of the line parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00054"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the point.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00055"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope of the line.</para>
      <para>Since the line is parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis, the line is horizontal. This means the slope of the line is 0.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00056"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into an equation.</para>
      <para>From here, you may know the equation, but if you don’t, then we can use the point-slope equation.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00058">Thus, the equation of the line parallel to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
     
      <para id="para-00060">Find the equation of the line perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis and passing through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      
      <para id="para-00061"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the point.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00062"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope of the line.</para>
      <para>Since the line is perpendicular to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis, the line is vertical. This means the slope of the line is undefined!</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mtext>undefined</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into an equation. Since the slope is undefined, we cannot use the point-slope equation. But, we know that if <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is on the line, then another point vertically above this point is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Another point vertically below this point is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. On a vertical line, the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-values do not change. So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00064">
        <emphasis>Example 3</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para id="para-00065">Find an equation of a line perpendicular to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> that contains the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
      <para id="para-00066">Again, since we know one point, the point-slope option seems more promising than the slope-intercept option. We need the slope to use this form, and we know the new line will be perpendicular to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. This line is vertical, so its perpendicular will be horizontal. This tells us the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msub><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mi>⟂</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msub><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the point.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00068"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope of the perpendicular line.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msub><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mi>⟂</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msub><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into an equation.</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00070"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify</para>
      <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      
      <para id="para-00071">
        <emphasis>Example 4</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para id="para-00072">Find an equation of a line that is perpendicular to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> that contains the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
      <para id="para-00073">The line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is a horizontal line. Any line perpendicular to it must be vertical, in the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Since the perpendicular line is vertical and passes through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, every point on it has an <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of 2. The equation of the perpendicular line is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Find Equations of Lines Perpendicular to an Axis</title>
        <!--Q#-->
      
          <exercise id="exer-00012">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00074">Find an equation of a line that is perpendicular to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> that contains the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00075">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00076">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00077">Here is how to find the equation of a line perpendicular to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> that contains the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00078"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope of the given line.  The line is horizontal. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope of the perpendicular line. The perpendicular line is a vertical line. undefined</para>
            <para id="para-00080"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Identify the point. The given point is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.   <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:msub><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:msub><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00081"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation. The perpendicular line is vertical and passes through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>; every point on it has an <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>